-- title:
--   Adic Galois representation of a newform, Steinberg Frobenius polynomials
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the sense of the project: $g$ is a normalised eigenform (its first $q$-expansion coefficient is $1$, its coefficients are multiplicative on coprime indices, and they satisfy the recursion $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_pa_{p^{r+1}}-p\,a_{p^r}$ for primes $p\nmid M$ and $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_pa_{p^{r+1}}$ for $p\mid M$), and for no proper divisor $M'$ of $M$ does there exist a normalised eigenform of weight two on $\Gamma_0(M')$ agreeing with $g$ in all coefficients $a_\ell$ at primes $\ell\nmid M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $S$ a finite set of naturals containing $\lambda$ and every prime divisor of $M$, and $O'$ a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation ring (a domain, adically complete for its maximal ideal) with finite residue field in which $\lambda$ lies in the maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(M)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$) and $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid M$, $q\notin S$), to $\mathbb{C}$, sending each such $T_\ell$ to the $\ell$-th $q$-coefficient of $g$; and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ to $O'$. Then there is a type $O''$, again a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field, which is an $O'$-algebra, module-finite over $O'$, whose structure map is a local homomorphism and is injective, together with an adic Galois representation $\rho$ over $O''$ — a free $O''$-module $V$ of rank two, finite over $O''$, with a monoid homomorphism from $\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $\operatorname{End}_{O''}(V)$ such that for every $n$ some finite subextension $L/\mathbb{Q}$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has all its fixing automorphisms acting trivially on $V$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}^n$ — satisfying two conditions. First, for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $A$, and every $\sigma$ lying in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and acting as $x\mapsto x^{\ell}$ on the residue field of $A$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X+\ell$ (image taken in $O''$). Second, for every prime $q\neq\lambda$ with $q\mid M$ and $q^2\nmid M$, and every such $A$ and Frobenius element $\sigma$ at $q$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is either $X^2-(q+1)X+q$ or $X^2+(q+1)X+q$.
--
--   This is the interface form of local–global compatibility for the $\lambda$-adic representation attached to a weight-two newform: the Eichler–Shimura relation at primes of good reduction outside $S$, and the Steinberg case at a prime exactly dividing the level, where the semisimplification is an unramified character times $1$ and the cyclotomic character and the unramified character takes the value $\pm 1$ at Frobenius. It is used in the construction of Hecke-eigenvalue-matching ring homomorphisms, in the analysis of inertia at a prime dividing the level exactly once, and in the passage from a newform to a modular model of the required level.
